{"name":"Honeysuckle Rose","hex":"#de8f8d","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"honeysuckle","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of the many species of arching shrubs and climbing vines of the genus Lonicera in the Caprifoliaceae family, many with sweet-smelling, bell-shaped flowers.\n\n A light yellow color inspired by the color of honeysuckle blossoms.","A light yellow color inspired by the color of honeysuckle blossoms."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/honeysuckle"},{"term":"rose","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A shrub of the genus Rosa, with red, pink, white or yellow flowers.","A flower of the rose plant."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make rose-colored; to redden or flush.","To perfume, as with roses."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/rose"}],"entries":[{"title":"Honeysuckle Rose (song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Honeysuckle Rose\" is a 1929 song composed by Thomas \"Fats\" Waller with lyrics by Andy Razaf. It was introduced in the 1929 Off-Broadway revue \"Load of Coal\" at Connie's Inn as a soft-shoe dance number. Waller's 1934 recording was inducted into the Grammy Hall of Fame in 1999."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Honeysuckle_rose.jpg/330px-Honeysuckle_rose.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Honeysuckle_Rose_(song)","wikidata":"Q280907"},{"title":"Honeysuckle Rose (film)","blocks":[{"text":"Honeysuckle Rose is a 1980 American romantic drama Western film directed by Jerry Schatzberg, written by John Binder, Gustaf Molander, Carol Sobieski, Gösta Stevens, and William D. Wittliff, and starring Willie Nelson, Dyan Cannon and Amy Irving. It is a loose remake of the 1936 Swedish film Intermezzo."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/f/fb/HoneysuckleRoseFilm.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Honeysuckle_Rose_(film)","wikidata":"Q2319060"},{"title":"Honeysuckle","blocks":[{"text":"Honeysuckles are arching shrubs or twining vines in the genus Lonicera of the family Caprifoliaceae. The genus includes 158 species native to northern latitudes in North America, Eurasia, and North Africa."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Lonicera_caprifolium001.jpg/330px-Lonicera_caprifolium001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Honeysuckle","wikidata":"Q156047"},{"title":"Rose","blocks":[{"text":"A rose is either a woody perennial flowering plant of the genus Rosa, in the family Rosaceae, or the flower it bears. There are over three hundred species and tens of thousands of cultivars. The plants range in size and form, from trailing and erect shrubs to climbers up to 7 metres (23 ft) in height. Sharp prickles appear on the stems of many species. Their flowers vary in size and shape and are usually large and showy, in colours ranging from white through pinks, reds, oranges and yellows."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Rosa_rubiginosa_1.jpg/330px-Rosa_rubiginosa_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rose","wikidata":"Q34687"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Honeysuckle-2.jpg/500px-Honeysuckle-2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Honeysuckle-2.jpg/1280px-Honeysuckle-2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Honeysuckle-2.jpg/500px-Honeysuckle-2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Honeysuckle (Lonicera japonica)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki